 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Women's employment in Victoria has grown over the past two years, but unemployment has risen since late 2025 and is now about the same as men's.\n\n**Unemployment (seasonally adjusted, September 2026):** 5.1% for women, up from 4.6% in October 2024. It was around 4.0% in late 2025 and peaked at 5.2% in July 2026. Men's rate for September 2026 was 5.0%. Through 2025, women's unemployment was lower than men's, so the gap has reversed.\n\n**Employment:** 1,843,000 women were employed in September 2026, up about 68,000 from October 2024. Employment has flattened since late 2025, though, and was roughly 1,835,000 to 1,844,000 in most months since October 2025.\n\n**Participation:** 63.6% for women in September 2026, slightly above 63.2% in October 2024. It dipped to 62.4% in April 2026 before recovering. For men it fell to 71.5% from 73.4% over the same period.\n\n**Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4% for women in September 2026, little changed from 60.3% in October 2024.\n\nMonthly survey estimates for a single state move around a lot, so the recent rise in unemployment is best read as a trend over several months, not a single data point.",
